---
import BlogPost from '$lib/layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import Provider from '$lib/openrouter/Provider.astro';
import ProviderDialogs from '$lib/openrouter/ProviderDialogs.astro';
import UsageChart from '$lib/openrouter/UsageChart.astro';
import homepage from './homepage.avif';
import models from './models.json';
import type { ProviderKey } from '$lib/openrouter/providers.ts';

export const title = 'Phala is a Chutes+ wrapper';
export const date = '2026-11-25';

const listfmt = new Intl.ListFormat('en-US', { style: 'long', type: 'conjunction' });
---

<BlogPost {title} {date}>
  <UsageChart of="phala" title="Phala's daily tokens on OpenRouter" />
  <p>
    Phala is a privacy focused AI API, and they've made it look an awful lot like they own the
    stack. Take a look at their <a
      href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121182306/https://phala.com/">homepage</a
    >:
  </p>
  <img {...homepage} />

  <h2>But Phala's API admits most of their models are wrappers</h2>
  {
    models.data.map((m) => (
      <p>
        <code>{m.id}</code> runs on{' '}
        {listfmt
          .formatToParts(m.providers)
          .map((part) =>
            part.type == 'element' ? <Provider of={part.value as ProviderKey} /> : part.value,
          )}
        .
      </p>
    ))
  }
  <p>
    This data is from <a
      href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121182013/https://inference.phala.com/v1/models"
      >https://inference.phala.com/v1/models</a
    >, which openly includes <code>"providers"</code> for each model. Notice how few are actually <Provider
      of="phala"
    />?
  </p>

  <h2>I'm glad Phala was removed from OpenRouter</h2>
  <p>
    When Phala was on OpenRouter, OpenRouter listed all models, even the ones Phala admitted simply
    went to another inference API. Phala saw this as an opportunity to make an extra buck by routing
    OpenRouter's Phala account to cheaper, non-TEE upstreams.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then Phala got hacked. In their <a
      href="https://phala.com/posts/phala-november-preliminary-incident-report">postmortem</a
    >, Phala admits OpenRouter users were sent down a different route than normal Phala users, and
    that this route logged request content. OpenRouter responded by <a
      href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261121184310/https://openrouter.ai/provider/phala"
      >completely delisting Phala</a
    >. I think this is a reasonable response to Phala's lack of honesty and security, and Phala
    should remove OpenRouter from their "Trusted by 5,000+ users" section.
  </p>
  <p>
    Phala's end-to-end encryption doesn't rule out sending you down a different route either. The
    machine you encrypt to is a Phala gateway with <a
      href="https://github.com/Dstack-TEE/private-ai-gateway/blob/9f3e693befca172c0672fc3af551cab253570540/src/http/app/backend.rs#L342-L345"
      >no GPU</a
    >, which decrypts your prompt and forwards it to an upstream from a list <a
      href="https://github.com/jooray/venice-e2ee-proxy/blob/b664f0ec0b7032a9eb8b039157c19ff9dbe48c75/README.md?plain=1#L279-L287"
      >Phala edits at runtime</a
    >, outside what the attestation covers. The same model can <a
      href="https://github.com/jooray/venice-e2ee-proxy/blob/b664f0ec0b7032a9eb8b039157c19ff9dbe48c75/README.md?plain=1#L313-L318"
      >land on Phala, Chutes or NEAR within the same hour</a
    >. I go into the rest of Phala's E2EE problems <a href="/blog/venicewrapper/#e2ee"
      >in my Venice post</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Disclosures</h2>
  <p>
    While a grant from OpenRouter helped with the research here and I work for an employer who would
    prefer to go unnamed, <strong
      >this post does not represent or reflect anyone else's opinion</strong
    > - it is simply research I am publishing.
  </p>
  <ProviderDialogs />
</BlogPost>
